rofessional mission statement which has  gone through several iterations but has  been fairly consistent and I now have it  as a three word three word mantra  education enlightenment evolution I  believe this process although lengthy of  learning your values and crafting your  personal mission and vision statement is  essential to leadership because no  matter where you are in your leadership  journey you can adapt this process to  help clarify and focus that journey and  get you to where you want to go second  define success for yourself now people  all around us are always ready to give  us their definitions of success from  numbers of publications you have to  appear in to the kind of home you have  to buy but women can define success for  ourselves and we really must third  embrace tradition but defy convention  there's a long history of women leaders  we can draw from and we can see how each  practice this principle in some way our  grandmothers have wisdom but we still  have to break new ground every day  anna-marie cal say president of the  university of washington has defied  conventions in  multiple ways she has been at the  institution for 30 years and has climbed  the ranks holding positions such as  provost executive vice president chairs  of two different departments and Dean of  the College of Arts and Sciences and  when she became president of Washington  she did not just become its president  she became its first Latina president  its first openly gay president and its  first female president quite a feat Cal  say grew up in Miami escaping with her  family from the Cuban Revolution at the  age of three she received both a  Bachelor of Arts in English and a  Bachelor of Science in psychology from  the University of Miami and eventually  went on to earn her PhD in psychology  from Yale  not only has cal state paved this  incredible path for herself but she has  made paving paths for others a priority  her concentration at Yale in fact was in  child clinical and community psychology  and she has dedicated much of her work  in that field to helping at-risk  adolescents particularly African  American and Latino youth as well as  homeless and street youth aside from her  research career diversity and closing  inequity gaps have been a really  important part of her agenda as  University administrator at Washington  Cal say created a faculty retention tool  kit some of Cal state's professional  development suggestions include  educating leaders about issues facing  women and minority faculty and having  informal lunches for STEM women faculty  she also offers examples of how one can  monitor equity in a university setting  and her presentations cal say recognizes  the importance of leadership and being a  woman leader in particular she managed  to keep these priorities in mind while  still actively carrying out her research  teaching and mentoring responsibilities  as she puts it quote I think the  importance of being a role model is not  at all trivial cal say believes that  education empowers people and improves  not only their lives but also the lives  of the people they touch and this is  especially the case for women  a special role they continue to play in  raising children whether alone or not I  want to end as I began by sharing the  wisdom of just some of the great women  leaders in higher education that Yale  has produced I spoke about the panel  discussion of former women university  presidents who had also been at Yale  that took place at our most recent  inauguration we can learn so much from  each other  Allison F Richard who served eight years  as yells Provost and as vice chancellor  for the University of Cambridge in  England reflected on how we have many  global exchange programs for  undergraduates such as that between  Cambridge and MIT but we often don't see  how we as leaders can learn from these  we should try to be more open to testing  what we ourselves are doing against what  others are doing she said such  relationships turn into institutional  learning opportunities they're not just  good for students and as former Penn  president Rodan points out with  universities being engines for  innovation one of the most important  qualities in a later in higher education  today is the ability to take risks  innovating in processes and  organizationally well today business  leaders even teach failure as being an  essential step in developing business  solutions and successful people take  risks shouldn't we women allow ourselves  this advantage the ability to fail and  to learn from our mistakes Susan  Hockfield a former Yale provost and  president of MIT for eight years calls  convening power the best part of  leadership and that women should use it  as often as possible she says that my  favorite day is being at a table with a  bunch of incredibly smart people  wrestling with the problem and someone  has an idea no one could have had on  their own well today I feel fortunate to  be in this room with you even if it's  virtually with a group of smart people  to consider how best to speak up  individually and collectively so that we  can all increase the number of women in  leadership positions in higher education  worldwide and I'm looking forward to  hearing all of you  questions and your stories and sharing  our voices on this important topic  because this is how we start pushing  that 30% figure in higher education  leadership to the 50% figure that it  really needs to be thank you and I'm  looking forward to your questions
 

